NAME
    wmcalc - calculator WM client (Path C phase 32, extended 156)

SYNOPSIS
    wmcalc [SECONDS]

DESCRIPTION
    Open a 220x368 fixed-point calculator window.  SECONDS is
    the lifetime cap (default 600 = 10 minutes); Ctrl-Q exits.

    Four-function calculator using two-register evaluation
    (pending op + accumulator).  Sequence "12.5 + 3.25 =" yields
    15.75; chained ops fold left-to-right.

    Values are stored as (mantissa, decimals) — fixed-point
    rather than integer (session 156).  '.' enters decimal-input
    mode; subsequent digits append to the fractional side.
    Trailing zeros are trimmed after every operation (so
    "1.50 + 0.50 =" displays as "2", not "2.00").

    Keys (delivered when the window has keyboard focus):
      0-9             digit
      .               start decimal-input mode
      + - * /         operator
      = or Enter      evaluate
      c or C          clear all (including memory)
      Backspace       drop the last digit of current entry
      _               toggle sign of current entry
      m               M+ (add current to memory)
      n               M- (subtract current from memory)
      r               MR (recall memory to current display)
      k               MC (clear memory)
      Ctrl-Q          quit

    Mouse: click any button.  Buttons briefly highlight when
    pressed.

    Indicators on the display panel:
      top-left "+/-/*//"     pending operator while chaining
      bottom-left "M"        non-zero memory register

    Memory row is the bottom row of the button grid (M+, M-,
    MR, MC).  M+ and M- update the memory register by adding
    or subtracting the current entry; MR replaces the current
    entry with the memory value; MC zeros the memory.

LIMITATIONS
    Range is signed int32: mantissa max 99999999 with one more
    multiply against another 9-digit operand still in range.
    Beyond that the display shows "ERR" in red.  Division
    keeps up to 4 extra decimal places of precision then
    truncates trailing zeros.  No scientific notation, no
    parentheses, no operator precedence — strictly two-
    register left-to-right (same as a pocket calculator).
    No M+= chaining buttons (M+= done by M+ then +/-).  The
    '.' button placeholder from the integer-only build
    (session 139) is now active.

